WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:03.703
NARRATOR: If tiger
sharks are showing up

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:06.706
in the shallows in
greater numbers,

00:00:06.773 --> 00:00:11.144
then it's not
because of deep blue.

00:00:11.211 --> 00:00:15.014
The reason for the
attacks remains elusive.

00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:18.318
But while scouring the
ocean for an explanation,

00:00:18.385 --> 00:00:20.520
experts come across
something else

00:00:20.587 --> 00:00:25.258
that also ensnares large
amounts of a tiger shark's prey.

00:00:25.325 --> 00:00:28.228
But it's not another
sea creature.

00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:32.365
In fact, it walks on two legs.

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:34.834
NAIA RAE FOX: I think the reason
why we are having more sharks

00:00:34.901 --> 00:00:36.936
here actually is because
the amount of fishing

00:00:37.003 --> 00:00:38.471
that's been going on.

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:40.740
NARRATOR: This is one of
the island's most popular

00:00:40.807 --> 00:00:45.044
activities, sport fishing.

00:00:45.111 --> 00:00:48.214
Every year, recreational
anglers haul in more

00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:51.751
than 2 million pounds of fish.

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:55.488
That means that on any given
day off the big island,

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:59.959
there are hundreds of fish
being caught and reeled in.

00:01:00.026 --> 00:01:02.929
And for scientists
like Dr. Dan Huber,

00:01:02.996 --> 00:01:08.101
these hooked sea creatures
may be attracting sharks.

00:01:08.168 --> 00:01:09.736
DAN HUBLER: A struggling
fish, as its body

00:01:09.803 --> 00:01:11.771
is twitching a bit,
that motion is giving

00:01:11.838 --> 00:01:13.440
off low frequency sounds.

00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:15.375
And shark hearing
happens to be tuned

00:01:15.442 --> 00:01:17.310
into low frequency sounds.

00:01:17.377 --> 00:01:19.512
This is definitely something
that could attract a number

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:22.182
of sharks to the same area.

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:25.051
NARRATOR: So could sport
fishing be drawing tiger sharks

00:01:25.118 --> 00:01:27.954
into the shallows,
thereby increasing

00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:30.290
the risk of an accidental
run in with people?

00:01:33.126 --> 00:01:35.328
According to
fisherman Rick Rieger,

00:01:35.395 --> 00:01:40.867
it comes down to where the
best sport fishing takes place.

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:44.037
To demonstrate, he heads
out to an area well

00:01:44.103 --> 00:01:45.405
known for the popular pastime.

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:52.812
RICK RIEGER: So this
is our bottom here.

00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:54.347
We have some of the
steepest drop offs

00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:56.983
in the whole world right here.

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:59.385
This is where you're going to
see your tuna, your mahi-mahi,

00:01:59.452 --> 00:02:02.021
your ono, your marlin.

00:02:02.088 --> 00:02:05.058
NARRATOR: This location
could hold a vital clue

00:02:05.124 --> 00:02:07.427
to solving the mystery.

00:02:07.494 --> 00:02:10.597
Called the pelagic
zone, this open water

00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:13.633
abyss extends thousands
of miles between

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.935
the continental shelves.

00:02:16.002 --> 00:02:20.106
It's home to an array
of large sea creatures--

00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:27.413
whales, manta rays, big game
fish, and some of the most

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:31.951
dangerous sharks on earth.

